  copyVersion: 4
  sourceSignature: 222d12c6eb83ba634b6e5d2918f567b7c730d398ec69b05f7390300ce348e459
  knowledge:
  - id: rule_of_thirds
    title: TECHNIQUE - RULE OF THIRDS
    description: 'LEVEL 2 TECHNIQUE


      - Divide the frame with the NONY FX Camera''s
      3 x 3 grid.

      - Place the product near the left or right vertical line
      instead of the center.

      - Put the most important detail close to a grid
      intersection.

      - Leave visual space in front of the direction a person
      faces or a vehicle points.

      - For Goke Cola, the product must sit clearly
      on the left or right third.


      WHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      The grid
      helps organize attention and open space; it is not a rule that every image
      must obey. Try centering, then move the subject toward an intersection. Notice
      how the empty space changes the balance. Keep the whole subject readable.'
    category: Technique
    level: 2
    sortOrder: 0
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: three_point_lighting
    title: TECHNIQUE - 3-POINT LIGHTING
    description: 'LEVEL 3 TECHNIQUE


      - KEY LIGHT: strongest, about 45 degrees
      to one side. Start near 75% intensity.

      - FILL LIGHT: opposite side controlling
      shadow depth. Start near 40%.

      - BACK LIGHT: behind the product for separation.
      Start near 60%; these percentages are practice examples, not universal targets.

      -
      Aim every beam at the product and adjust tilt until the light reaches it.

      -
      Check the camera image for depth, readable highlights, and controlled shadows.


      WHY
      IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Judge each light by its job. Compare key alone,
      key plus fill, then all three. The key establishes shape; fill reveals shadows
      without erasing them; back light defines an edge. If adding fill makes the
      image flat, reduce it. Percentages depend on distance and aim.'
    category: Technique
    level: 3
    sortOrder: 10
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: director_tablet
    title: EQUIPMENT - DIRECTOR TABLET
    description: 'PURPOSE

      The Director Tablet builds the physical set: backdrop,
      props and actors. It controls placement; the megaphone controls actor performance.


      HOW
      TO USE

      Press [E] at the terminal. Early levels use ADD WALL; from Level
      4, CHOOSE SET offers interiors. Select the wall to change its color with RGB
      sliders, number fields or HEX.

      Click an approved item card, move the
      preview, then click to place. Select an object and press [T] to reposition
      it. CLEAR STAGE removes the current setup.


      PLAN BEFORE BUYING

      Check
      the contract first. Props and sets cost money. Leave space for the camera and
      lights, keep important products unobstructed, and check the set through the
      viewfinder. Close the tablet before using handheld equipment.'
    category: Equipment
    level: 1
    sortOrder: 0
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: led_panel
    title: EQUIPMENT - 160 LED PANEL
    description: 'PURPOSE

      The 160 LED Panel is your basic portable light.
      Use it to make a subject readable and reveal its shape. Position and aim matter
      as much as intensity.


      HOW TO USE

      Equip the light. [LMB] switches
      power; [Scroll] changes intensity; [Up/Down] changes tilt. Hold [Q] to raise
      or [E] to lower the stand. [G] places it.


      CHECK THE RESULT

      Aim
      at the subject, not only the wall. Move to one side to reveal a bright side
      and a shadow side. Increase output if detail is too dark; reduce it if detail
      disappears in bright areas. Check through the camera before recording.


      The
      setup beam is an aiming aid, not part of the recorded image. This light is
      simpler and lower-output than Better Lights.'
    category: Equipment
    level: 1
    sortOrder: 10
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: nony_fx_camera
    title: EQUIPMENT - NONY FX CAMERA
    description: 'PURPOSE

      The NONY FX Camera chooses what the audience sees
      and records it. Composition, movement and exposure affect how clearly the subject
      reads.


      HOW TO USE

      Carry a blank SD Card and press [C] to insert
      it. [LMB] opens/exits the viewfinder. [Scroll] zooms; [Q/E] adjusts height.
      Hold [Ctrl] for controlled movement. [R] starts/stops recording; collect the
      ejected card afterward.


      SETTINGS

      From Level 2, [F2] opens/closes
      settings. Up/Down selects a row; Left/Right changes it. Close settings before
      composing. Focus stays automatic. The thirds grid supports composition; Level
      3 adds white balance; Level 4 adds exposure controls.


      CHECK THE RESULT

      Keep
      the subject visible, leave intentional space, and avoid accidental camera movement.
      Finish zoom and height adjustments before the take. Review recorded footage
      at the computer.'
    category: Equipment
    level: 1
    sortOrder: 20
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: sd_card
    title: EQUIPMENT - SD CARD
    description: 'PURPOSE

      An SD Card carries a recorded take from the camera
      to the computer. A blank card is recording storage; a used card contains footage
      and its production results.


      HOW TO USE

      1. Keep a blank card
      in the hotbar. Hold the camera and press [C] to insert it.

      2. Record
      with [R], then stop. Collect the used card with [E].

      3. Hold that card
      at the computer tower and press [F] to ingest. Open the monitor with [E] to
      review the take.


      GOOD HABIT

      Prepare enough blank cards for separate
      takes. Review framing and subject visibility before editing: color grading
      cannot restore a subject that was outside the frame.'
    category: Equipment
    level: 1
    sortOrder: 30
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: level_3_soft_light
    title: EQUIPMENT - BETTER LIGHTS
    description: 'PURPOSE

      Better Lights provide higher output plus temperature
      and diffusion controls. Use them to shape reflective products and build key,
      fill and back lighting.


      HOW TO USE

      [LMB] power; [Scroll] intensity;
      [Up/Down] tilt; hold [Q/E] to raise/lower; [G] place. [Z/X] adjusts Kelvin.
      [V/B] adjusts diffusion.


      UNDERSTAND THE CONTROLS

      Lower Kelvin
      makes the source warmer; higher Kelvin makes it cooler. Diffusion softens the
      light''s shadow transition. It is not a brightness control.


      CHECK THE
      RESULT

      Use a stronger key to create shape, a gentler fill to reveal
      shadow detail, and a rear light to define the edge. Match source temperatures
      for a consistent starting point. Change diffusion for each light''s job; 3200K
      and 75% are not universal requirements. Compare in the camera and preserve
      visible detail.'
    category: Equipment
    level: 3
    sortOrder: 0
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: quiet_movement
    title: TECHNIQUE - QUIET MOVEMENT ON SET
    description: 'Hold either CTRL key with WASD to walk slowly without footstep
      sounds, with any equipment or empty hands. CTRL takes priority over SHIFT sprint.
      Release CTRL to return to normal movement. When holding a camera, CTRL also
      keeps the existing gentle mouse look and eased movement for smooth shots. Jump
      landings still make sound.


      WHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Quiet walking
      reduces unwanted footstep noise, but is not silent jumping. Move deliberately
      during a take and avoid unnecessary movement near the recorded action.'
    category: Technique
    level: 1
    sortOrder: 5
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: actor_megaphone
    title: EQUIPMENT - DIRECTOR MEGAPHONE
    description: 'PURPOSE

      The Director Megaphone cues hired actors. It does
      not control cameras, lights or editing. Hire and place actors with the tablet
      first.


      HOW TO USE

      Equip the megaphone and click an actor to
      select them. [Z] cycles Neutral, Wave and Action. Action uses coffee when the
      actor holds a cup. Aim at a stool or coffee machine and click to cue its interaction.
      [O] stops the action.

      [T] repositions; [R] turns the actor in steps;
      arrow keys nudge placement. [B/N] stores walk marks, [K] rehearses, [J] returns
      to START, and [H] clears the route.


      CHECK THE PERFORMANCE

      Rehearse
      before recording. Keep the actor and relevant product visible without blocking
      each other. Check the pose and screen direction between matching shots. More
      expensive actors change performance polish, not the basic contract requirements.'
    category: Equipment
    level: 4
    sortOrder: 5
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: camera_white_balance
    title: CAMERA UNLOCK - WHITE BALANCE
    description: 'Level 3: F2, select White balance with Up/Down, then Left/Right
      changes Kelvin. Lower camera Kelvin makes the image cooler; higher makes it
      warmer. Tint adjusts green/magenta. Camera white balance changes the recorded
      image; light Kelvin changes the light itself. Try matching a 3200K source,
      then choose a deliberate warm look.


      WHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      White
      balance compensates for the light''s color; it does not reposition or brighten
      the light. Look at a neutral surface while changing the setting. Compare the
      warm and cool casts, then choose a neutral starting point or an intentional
      mood.'
    category: Technique
    level: 3
    sortOrder: 25
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: camera_exposure
    title: CAMERA UNLOCK - EXPOSURE
    description: 'Level 4: F2 reveals ISO, Aperture and Shutter angle. Up/Down selects;
      Left/Right adjusts. Higher ISO brightens the image. A lower F-number brightens
      it and reduces depth of field. A wider shutter angle increases exposure and
      the simulated motion blur. Starting point: ISO 800, F4, 180 degrees at 24fps
      (about 1/48 second). These controls are a teaching simulation, not Sony sensor
      emulation. Settings affect the viewfinder and recorded footage.


      WHY
      IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Exposure controls how light or dark the recorded
      image is. Change one setting at a time and watch detail in highlights and shadows.
      A bright image is not automatically a better image: keep the product''s texture
      and shape readable.'
    category: Technique
    level: 4
    sortOrder: 5
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: set_building_technique
    title: TECHNIQUE - SET BUILDING & PRODUCT STAGING
    description: 'LEVEL 1 TECHNIQUE


      - Match the backdrop color and approved
      props to the contract brief.

      - Use a support cube when the product needs
      height.

      - Keep the product visible and remove anything blocking its
      silhouette.

      - In the Flower Vase contract, use a pink backdrop, place
      the cube near center, then place the flower on top.

      - Confirm the final
      placement through the camera viewfinder, not only from the player view.


      WHY
      IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Build for the camera''s view. Compare silhouettes
      and overlaps, leave useful negative space, and remove clutter. Moving the subject
      away from the backdrop gives you more control over its shadow and separation.'
    category: Technique
    level: 1
    sortOrder: 0
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: center_framing
    title: TECHNIQUE - CENTER FRAMING
    description: 'LEVEL 1 TECHNIQUE


      - Place the main subject in the middle
      of the frame.

      - Keep it inside the center area for the entire take.

      -
      Use zoom to reduce distracting background and [Q/E] to correct camera height
      before recording.

      - Center framing creates a simple, direct product
      image and is required by the Flower Vase contract.

      - Do not move the
      camera during the 10-second recording.


      WHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Centering
      gives a subject direct emphasis. Compare both sides of the frame: distracting
      empty space or a cropped edge can weaken the shot. Check the entire take, not
      just its first frame.'
    category: Technique
    level: 1
    sortOrder: 10
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: basic_product_lighting
    title: TECHNIQUE - BASIC PRODUCT LIGHTING
    description: 'LEVEL 1 TECHNIQUE


      - Use one powered light to illuminate
      the front or side of the product.

      - Aim the light before dropping it
      and confirm the beam reaches the subject.

      - For the Flower Vase contract,
      set intensity to 45% and tilt to -5 degrees.

      - Increase intensity when
      the subject is too dark; reduce it when highlights lose detail.

      - Check
      the result through the camera viewfinder before recording.


      WHY IT MATTERS
      / PRACTICE

      A well-lit image still needs shadows to show form. Compare
      front lighting with lighting from one side. Check detail in both the brightest
      and darkest areas before recording.'
    category: Technique
    level: 1
    sortOrder: 20
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: recording_technique
    title: TECHNIQUE - STABLE 10-SECOND RECORDING
    description: 'LEVEL 1 TECHNIQUE


      - Insert a blank SD Card and finish the
      composition before pressing [R].

      - Keep the camera stable and the subject
      correctly framed throughout the take.

      - Record for the duration requested
      by the contract; the training and Goke contracts use 10 seconds.

      - Press
      [R] again to stop and generate the used SD Card.

      - Review the ingested
      clip before opening the Editor.


      WHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Begin
      only after framing and lighting are ready. Avoid an accidental wobble at the
      start or finish. Review the take before editing; an unusable recording is better
      replaced than hidden with effects.'
    category: Technique
    level: 1
    sortOrder: 30
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: post_production_technique
    title: TECHNIQUE - TRIMMING, BRANDING & COLOR
    description: 'LEVEL 1 TECHNIQUE


      - Drag the clip to the Video Track, trim
      it to 10.0 seconds, and move it to 0.0 seconds.

      - Keep Logo 1 on screen
      from 0-5 seconds and Logo 2 from 5-10 seconds without blocking the product.

      -
      Use brightness for exposure, contrast for separation, and saturation for color
      strength.

      - Choose your own Flower Vase look. All available values earn
      full color credit: Brightness 0.75-1.25, Contrast 0.75-1.50, Saturation 0.65-1.40.
      Compare Before / After and keep product detail readable.

      - Export, review
      the final render, then submit it.


      WHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Editing
      controls order and timing. Trim dead time, keep a continuous sequence, and
      let graphics support rather than cover the product. Preview from the beginning
      before export to catch gaps and mistimed branding.'
    category: Technique
    level: 1
    sortOrder: 40
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: product_separation
    title: TECHNIQUE - PRODUCT & BACKDROP SEPARATION
    description: 'LEVEL 2 TECHNIQUE


      - Pull the product forward instead of
      leaving it against the backdrop.

      - Physical distance creates depth and
      gives the Back Light room to work.

      - Keep the product silhouette clear
      from props with similar colors.

      - For Goke Cola, use a red backdrop
      and place the can wherever you choose; no minimum wall distance is required.

      -
      Use lighting and color contrast to guide attention toward the product.


      WHY
      IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Build for the camera''s view. Compare silhouettes
      and overlaps, leave useful negative space, and remove clutter. Moving the subject
      away from the backdrop gives you more control over its shadow and separation.'
    category: Technique
    level: 2
    sortOrder: 20
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: commercial_color_grading
    title: TECHNIQUE - COMMERCIAL COLOR GRADING
    description: 'OPTIONAL GOKE FINISH


      Brightness controls exposure, contrast
      separates light and dark areas, and saturation controls color strength. Preserve
      highlight and shadow detail. For this contract these controls are optional;
      the lesson focuses on intro/outro placement. The contract also requires two
      overlays, with timing and duration chosen by you.


      WHY IT MATTERS /
      PRACTICE

      Brightness changes overall lightness, contrast changes separation
      between dark and light, and saturation changes color strength. Change one at
      a time and compare before/after. Preserve detail and match neighboring shots
      instead of maximizing every slider.'
    category: Technique
    level: 2
    sortOrder: 30
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: advertising_post_production
    title: TECHNIQUE - INTRO & OUTRO
    description: 'GOKE POST-PRODUCTION


      INTRO: Introduces the brand and sets
      the tone, helping viewers understand whose commercial they are watching.


      OUTRO:
      Reinforces the brand and leaves a memorable closing message. ''Make it a Goke''
      invites the viewer to choose the product.


      Both clips are supplied in
      CLIPS. Drag the full 2-second intro to 0s, place 8 seconds of your recorded
      product footage after it, and finish with the full 2-second outro at 10s. Join
      all clips without gaps or overlaps. Double-click footage to trim; right-click
      a clip to return it to the bank. Use both the Goke logo and tagline overlays.
      Choose when and how long they appear; no fixed order or minimum hold is required.
      Preview the complete 12-second commercial before export.


      WHY IT MATTERS
      / PRACTICE

      The intro establishes brand identity; the outro leaves a
      final message. Give the product footage enough time between them. Preview all
      three parts together: a readable beginning, middle and ending matters more
      than adding effects.'
    category: Technique
    level: 2
    sortOrder: 40
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: hiring_and_posing_actors
    title: TECHNIQUE - HIRING, BLOCKING & POSING ACTORS
    description: 'LEVEL 4 TECHNIQUE


      - Click an Actor card, move the actor
      over the stage, then click again to place them. Hire prices reflect acting
      polish: Rookie 750, Trained 2,250, Expert 4,500 B-Coins (default rates).

      -
      Place and reposition with the tablet. Equip the megaphone and click the actor
      to select them.

      - Press [Z] to cycle Neutral, Wave, Action, then Neutral.
      Aim at a stool or machine and click to cue Sitting or Using Machine; [O] stops
      it. Bots perform automatically on their mark; they restart the action for every
      take. Rookie gestures are smaller with slower cues; Trained and Expert actors
      deliver progressively smoother, more expressive gestures. All tiers can meet
      the contract.

      - Select the actor and press [T] to reposition them; [R]
      turns the bot in 15-degree steps.

      - Block the actor beside the product
      without hiding its important shape.

      - Preserve the same pose and screen
      side across matching shots.


      WHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Blocking
      means placing the actor and planning movement so the action reads on camera.
      Rehearse first. Check that furniture, props and the actor''s body do not hide
      the product at the important moment.'
    category: Technique
    level: 4
    sortOrder: 0
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: automotive_staging
    title: TECHNIQUE - AUTOMOTIVE STAGING & COMPOSITION
    description: 'LEVEL 3 TECHNIQUE


      - Show a readable front or side silhouette
      of the vehicle.

      - Leave open space around the body instead of crowding
      it with props.

      - Use the Rule of Thirds grid to balance the vehicle
      with intentional negative space.

      - Aim the Soft Light across the body
      to reveal form without clipping reflections.

      - Check that the vehicle
      direction and empty space guide the viewer through the frame.


      WHY IT
      MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Build for the camera''s view. Compare silhouettes
      and overlaps, leave useful negative space, and remove clutter. Moving the subject
      away from the backdrop gives you more control over its shadow and separation.'
    category: Technique
    level: 3
    sortOrder: 10
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: soft_light_technique
    title: TECHNIQUE - SOFT LIGHTING FOR REFLECTIVE SURFACES
    description: 'LEVEL 3 TECHNIQUE


      - Move the Better Lights across the front
      or side of the vehicle to reveal body shape.

      - Start near 75% intensity
      and -10 degrees tilt, then aim the beam across the car.

      - Change distance
      and intensity together: farther placement widens coverage but reduces brightness.

      -
      Keep enough shadow to preserve depth instead of lighting every surface equally.

      -
      In post use Contrast 1.15-1.45, Saturation 0.95-1.20, and Brightness 0.90-1.10.


      WHY
      IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Watch the transition from bright to dark on the
      subject. Change diffusion and compare the transition, then adjust intensity
      separately. On reflective surfaces, move the light until the highlight describes
      the shape instead of hiding it.'
    category: Technique
    level: 3
    sortOrder: 20
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: coffee_story_workflow
    title: LEVEL 4 - COFFEE PRODUCT COMMERCIAL
    description: "Film the cup of coffee and Kape packaging together for a product
      overview. Then record an actor using coffee in a coffee-shop interior. Give
      the actor a cup and cue Action, or use the coffee machine with coffee in frame.
      Build a continuous 30\u201345-second edit, beginning with the overview. Keep
      at least 2 seconds of each required shot. No overlays. Additional shots, lighting
      and color choices are yours.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE\nPlan the overview
      and use shot before recording. Review the required subjects in each take, then
      assemble the edit. Extra shots should clarify the product story, not replace
      the required evidence."
    category: Technique
    level: 4
    sortOrder: 10
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: shot_coverage
    title: TECHNIQUE - PRODUCT AND PERFORMANCE
    description: "Introduce the coffee with its packaging, then show its use in the
      cafe. Keep both cup and packaging visible in the overview. In the performance
      shot, keep actor and coffee readable. Record enough material to choose the
      strongest 30\u201345 seconds.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE\nAn overview answers
      WHAT the product is; a use shot answers HOW it belongs in the scene. Record
      both clearly so the audience understands the connection. Keep product and actor
      visible when the action matters."
    category: Technique
    level: 4
    sortOrder: 20
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: screen_continuity
    title: TECHNIQUE - SPLITTING AND TRIMMING
    description: 'Click a timeline clip, then press B to split it at the red playhead.
      The two halves retain their footage and color settings and can be edited separately.
      Double-click to trim. Remove unwanted waiting and join clips without gaps or
      overlaps.


      WHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Splitting creates two editable
      pieces; trimming removes unwanted time. Neither creates new footage. Watch
      across each join for a readable action and check for gaps or overlaps. Preserve
      enough time for the viewer to understand each shot.'
    category: Technique
    level: 4
    sortOrder: 30
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: motivated_lighting
    title: TECHNIQUE - STORY MOOD
    description: 'Choose lighting and set color to support your character''s coffee
      break. There is no required light model, Kelvin value, or brown paint in this
      contract. Check subject readability in your preview.


      WHY IT MATTERS
      / PRACTICE

      Choose a mood, then check that it still serves readability.
      Warm or cool color alone does not tell the story: direction, shadow depth and
      clear performance also matter.'
    category: Technique
    level: 4
    sortOrder: 40
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: lifestyle_staging
    title: TECHNIQUE - PERFORMANCE SPACE
    description: 'Give one lead actor a readable space beside the coffee product
      and chair. Keep furniture clear of the performance. Use the megaphone to cue
      each moment. Existing coffee-machine interactions are optional discovery, not
      a new tutorial requirement.


      WHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Blocking
      means placing the actor and planning movement so the action reads on camera.
      Rehearse first. Check that furniture, props and the actor''s body do not hide
      the product at the important moment.'
    category: Technique
    level: 4
    sortOrder: 50
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: warm_commercial_grade
    title: TECHNIQUE - CLIP COLOR GRADING
    description: "Click a timeline clip and open COLOR GRADE. Brightness, contrast
      and saturation affect only that clip in Level 4. Select another clip to edit
      its own settings. Preview the entire 30\u201345-second commercial for consistency.
      Do not add overlays for this contract.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE\nBrightness
      changes overall lightness, contrast changes separation between dark and light,
      and saturation changes color strength. Change one at a time and compare before/after.
      Preserve detail and match neighboring shots instead of maximizing every slider."
    category: Technique
    level: 4
    sortOrder: 60
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: creative_brief
    title: TECHNIQUE - INTERPRETING A CREATIVE BRIEF
    description: 'LEVEL 5 TECHNIQUE


      - Identify the audience, communication
      goal, required subjects, mood, and deliverables before building the set.

      -
      Translate each written requirement into a visible production decision.

      -
      For Haraya, the teal campaign world, actor, product, and vehicle must feel
      like one intentional brand story.

      - A creative choice can break a composition
      convention only when it still serves the brief and remains readable.


      WHY
      IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Separate required deliverables from creative choices.
      Make a short checklist of subjects, duration and finish, then decide how to
      communicate the idea. Review the checklist before spending and before submitting.'
    category: Technique
    level: 5
    sortOrder: 0
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: visual_hierarchy
    title: TECHNIQUE - INTEGRATED CAMPAIGN & VISUAL HIERARCHY
    description: 'LEVEL 5 TECHNIQUE


      - Visual hierarchy controls what the
      viewer notices first, second, and third.

      - Use scale, contrast, placement,
      light, and negative space to make the product dominant while the actor and
      vehicle provide context.

      - Carry one brand idea through production design,
      performance, composition, lighting, graphics, and color.

      - Prevent tangencies,
      overlaps, and background clutter from weakening silhouettes.

      - Record
      at least four purposeful shots using three shot sizes so the final edit has
      progression and variety.


      WHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Ask where
      your eye goes first. If a background prop is more noticeable than the product,
      simplify the frame or change placement and contrast. Each shot should have
      an intentional main point of interest.'
    category: Technique
    level: 5
    sortOrder: 10
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: quality_control
    title: TECHNIQUE - COMMERCIAL QUALITY CONTROL
    description: 'LEVEL 5 TECHNIQUE


      - Review the brief before recording,
      before export, and before submission.

      - Confirm required subjects, shot
      variety, lighting roles, duration, graphic count, and color ranges.

      -
      Watch the finished sequence for empty frames, accidental reversals, obstructed
      products, mismatched shots, or unreadable graphics.

      - Haraya requires
      a 20-second edit, three graphics, three-point lighting, and a polished grade
      within the contract qualifications.


      WHY IT MATTERS / PRACTICE

      Watch
      once for story, once for image problems, and once against the brief. Check
      transitions, readable branding and final duration. Fix errors before export
      and review the exported result too.'
    category: Technique
    level: 5
    sortOrder: 20
    isUnlocked: 0
  - id: editing_computer
    title: EQUIPMENT - EDITING COMPUTER
    description: 'PURPOSE

      Turn recorded takes into a finished commercial.
      The tower ingests footage; the monitor lets you review, edit and submit it.


      HOW
      TO USE

      1. Collect the used SD Card after recording. Hold it at the tower
      and press [F] to ingest.

      2. Press [E] at the monitor. Review RECORDINGS,
      then enter the Editor.

      3. Drag footage from the bin to the Video Track.
      Arrange clips from 0 seconds. Double-click to trim unwanted footage.

      4.
      Preview the sequence, export, watch the result, then submit.


      TOOLS

      EDITOR
      arranges and trims shots. BRANDING adds and times graphics when the brief asks
      for them. COLOR GRADE adjusts brightness, contrast and saturation. EXPORT produces
      the finished sequence.


      From Level 4, select a timeline clip to grade
      only that clip; [B] splits the selected clip at the red playhead. Follow the
      active brief for duration and overlays.'
    category: Equipment
    level: 1
    sortOrder: 40
    isUnlocked: 0
